Use case:
A framework built on top of JAX defines an array-like class with binary operators that allow it to be api-compatible with jax's DeviceArray
class NewThingy():

  def __add__(self, device_array):
   ...
  def __radd__(self, device_array):
   ...

FYI: __radd__ is the reflected version of __add__, which is meant to be called when the second operand of the "+" operator is a NewThingy.
Currently this does not work because JAX does not respect the standard way of implementing type handling in binary operators.  In the case where the left operand is a DeviceArray and the right operand is a NewThingy, the python interpreter first tries to call DeviceArray.__add__, and if the method cannot handle the other operand (because it is of an incompatible type), then the interpreter will try the reflected operator provided by NewThingy.__radd__.  For this to work, DeviceArray.__add__ needs to return NotImplemented when the second operand is incompatible.  Instead, the current implementation raises  a TypeError which prevents Python's operator reflection mechanism from kicking in.
To fix this, we would need the functions created by _one_to_one_binop and _maybe_bool_binop (in numpy/lax_numpy.py) to return NotImplemented whenever _check_arraylike detects an incompatible argument.

